# UPGRADE-10

# Enterprise SaaS Control Plane

# Hard Multi-Tenancy, Organizations/Projects,

# SSO/SCIM, Service Accounts, Policy-as-Code,

# Quotas/Metering/Billing Boundary & Enterprise Audit

You are working directly inside ZCoder after Upgrade-09.

Reported baseline includes:

* PostgreSQL production backend
* real multi-process PostgreSQL coordination
* atomic claims
* fencing
* worker recovery
* GitHub App fleet
* containerization
* Docker Compose
* Kubernetes / Helm
* OIDC/RBAC
* OpenTelemetry
* backup / restore
* production release gate
* approximately 694 passing tests
* two optional skips
* version still reported as 1.40.0

DO NOT trust any of these claims until verified.

This upgrade has two distinct missions:

1. correct remaining production-evidence overclaims from Upgrade-09;
2. convert ZCoder from a production-capable single-organization control plane into an enterprise SaaS-ready architecture with hard tenant boundaries.

Do not confuse:

multi-repository

with:

multi-tenant SaaS.

They are different security models.

================================================================
PHASE 0 — FORENSICALLY VERIFY UPGRADE-09
========================================

Run first:

```bash
git status --short
git log -30 --oneline
git tag --sort=-version:refname | head -30

python -m pytest -q
python main.py --version
python main.py --help
python -m build
git diff --check
```

Inspect:

```text
postgres_store.py
control_plane.py
worker_process.py
production_config.py
auth_oidc.py
observability_otel.py
backup_restore.py
release_gate.py
deploy/
.github/workflows/
tests/
docs/
```

Produce:

```text
UPGRADE-09 EVIDENCE AUDIT

Capability
Previous Claim
Evidence Actually Executed
Environment
Limitations
Correct Verdict
```

Verdicts:

```text
PROVEN
PROVEN_WITH_LIMITATION
CONFIGURED_NOT_EXECUTED
SIMULATED
UNIT_ONLY
MOCK_ONLY
DOCUMENTED_ONLY
NOT_PROVEN
FALSE_POSITIVE
```

Never convert CONFIGURED_NOT_EXECUTED into PASS.

================================================================

1. CORRECT THE FINAL VERDICT
   ================================================================

Upgrade-09 reportedly contains:

```text
PITR: PROVEN_WITH_LIMITATION
```

and also reports limitations around:

* multi-host simulation
* external PostgreSQL HA
* external durable WAL archive

Ensure the final verdict accurately reflects those limitations.

A report must never conclude:

```text
FULLY VERIFIED
```

when mandatory or explicitly claimed properties remain limited.

Introduce evidence-aware status:

```text
PASS
PASS_WITH_LIMITATIONS
FAIL
UNKNOWN
```

Use:

```text
PASS_WITH_LIMITATIONS
```

where appropriate.

================================================================
2. RELEASE-GATE EVIDENCE MODEL
==============================

Every gate must contain evidence, not just a boolean.

Concept:

```python
GateEvidence:
    gate
    status
    command
    test
    environment
    timestamp
    artifact
    limitations
```

A gate cannot become PASS merely because:

```python
gate = True
```

exists in source.

================================================================
3. VERSION DISCIPLINE
=====================

The project has reportedly accumulated:

Upgrade-04
Upgrade-05
Upgrade-06
Upgrade-07
Upgrade-08
Upgrade-09

while still identifying itself as:

```text
1.40.0
```

Investigate actual:

* Git tags
* GitHub releases
* PyPI releases
* container tags
* public API compatibility
* CLI compatibility

Do not fabricate release history.

Choose a sane release strategy.

If 1.40.0 has never been published, it is permissible to redefine the
pending release, but explicitly document why.

If public compatibility has materially changed, evaluate whether a
major version is warranted.

Do not choose v2 merely because the project became larger.

Use actual SemVer reasoning.

================================================================
4. DEFINE THE TENANT MODEL
==========================

Introduce explicit hierarchy.

Recommended conceptual model:

```text
Platform
  │
  ├── Organization
  │     │
  │     ├── Project
  │     │     │
  │     │     ├── Repository
  │     │     ├── Jobs
  │     │     ├── Agents
  │     │     ├── Policies
  │     │     └── Usage
  │     │
  │     ├── Members
  │     ├── Groups
  │     ├── Service Accounts
  │     ├── API Keys
  │     ├── GitHub Installations
  │     └── Billing Account
```

Do not use GitHub organization as ZCoder tenant identity.

GitHub is an integration.

ZCoder organization is the security/billing/administrative boundary.

================================================================
5. TENANT IDENTIFIERS
=====================

Every tenant-owned domain entity must have an immutable tenant scope.

Examples:

```text
organization_id
project_id
```

Audit:

jobs
events
approvals
workers where applicable
repositories
GitHub installations
PR mappings
artifacts
usage
budgets
policies
audit records
API keys
service accounts
webhooks
outbox
memory references
agent profiles

Do not infer tenant by joining through several optional tables when a
direct security boundary is appropriate.

================================================================
6. TENANT CONTEXT
=================

Introduce a first-class authenticated request context.

Concept:

```python
RequestContext:
    principal_id
    organization_id
    project_id
    roles
    permissions
    authentication_method
```

Do not pass organization IDs independently from authenticated identity
without authorization validation.

================================================================
7. ZERO TRUST FOR CLIENT TENANT IDS
===================================

The API must never trust:

```text
X-Organization-ID
organization_id in JSON
project_id in URL
```

merely because the client sent it.

Every requested tenant/project scope must be checked against the
authenticated principal.

================================================================
8. APPLICATION-LAYER TENANT ISOLATION
=====================================

Every store/service API that accesses tenant-owned data should require
explicit scope.

Bad:

```python
get_job(job_id)
```

Preferred:

```python
get_job(context, job_id)
```

or an architecture providing equivalent enforced tenant context.

Prevent accidental global queries.

================================================================
9. POSTGRESQL ROW-LEVEL SECURITY
================================

Evaluate PostgreSQL Row-Level Security as defense-in-depth.

Verify current PostgreSQL behavior from official documentation before
implementing.

For tenant-owned tables, consider:

```sql
ALTER TABLE ... ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE ... FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
```

where appropriate.

Create policies for:

```text
SELECT
INSERT
UPDATE
DELETE
```

Do not rely solely on SELECT policy.

================================================================
10. RLS ROLE SAFETY
===================

Explicitly verify:

* application DB role
* migration role
* table owner
* BYPASSRLS
* administrative maintenance paths

The normal application role must not accidentally bypass tenant RLS.

Migration/admin roles must remain distinct.

================================================================
11. DATABASE SESSION TENANT CONTEXT
===================================

If RLS uses session-local tenant variables:

set them inside a controlled transaction boundary.

Example conceptual mechanism:

```text
SET LOCAL application.organization_id = ...
```

Verify exact safe PostgreSQL implementation.

Do not leave tenant context attached to a pooled connection after the
request.

================================================================
12. CONNECTION POOL TENANT LEAK TEST
====================================

Mandatory test:

Request A:
organization A

connection returned to pool

Request B:
organization B

same physical DB connection

Prove B cannot observe A's tenant context or data.

This is P0.

================================================================
13. RLS NEGATIVE TEST MATRIX
============================

For every tenant-owned table:

Create:

Organization A
Organization B

Insert records for both.

Then deliberately attempt:

A reads B
A updates B
A deletes B
A inserts using B tenant ID

Every attempt must fail or return no inaccessible record according to
API contract.

Positive tests are insufficient.

================================================================
14. RAW SQL ESCAPE AUDIT
========================

Search for all direct SQL.

Identify queries capable of bypassing normal scoped repositories.

No maintenance helper should silently become a tenant escape hatch.

================================================================
15. DATABASE BACKUP + RLS
=========================

Verify backup/restore behavior with RLS.

Do not assume application RLS roles are suitable for backup.

Document privileged backup role separately.

Ensure restored policies and tenant constraints remain present.

================================================================
16. ORGANIZATIONS
=================

Implement lifecycle:

```text
ACTIVE
SUSPENDED
DELETING
DELETED
```

Support:

create
read
update
suspend
reactivate where policy allows

Do not implement immediate destructive hard deletion for enterprise
tenant data without retention policy.

================================================================
17. PROJECTS
============

Projects are subdivisions of an organization.

Support:

create
list
update
archive

Project isolation must never override organization boundary.

================================================================
18. MEMBERSHIP
==============

Implement explicit organization membership.

Concept:

```python
Membership:
    principal_id
    organization_id
    role
    status
```

States:

```text
INVITED
ACTIVE
SUSPENDED
REMOVED
```

================================================================
19. ENTERPRISE RBAC
===================

Expand simple global:

```text
VIEWER
OPERATOR
ADMIN
```

into scoped roles.

Possible initial model:

```text
OrganizationOwner
OrganizationAdmin
ProjectAdmin
Operator
Developer
Viewer
BillingAdmin
SecurityAuditor
```

Do not hardcode UI behavior as authorization.

Server-side permission evaluation is mandatory.

================================================================
20. PERMISSION MODEL
====================

Represent concrete permissions separately from role names.

Examples:

```text
org.read
org.manage
member.read
member.manage

project.read
project.manage

job.read
job.create
job.cancel
job.approve

repo.read
repo.manage

policy.read
policy.manage

billing.read
billing.manage

audit.read
```

Role -> permission mapping should be explicit and testable.

================================================================
21. RESOURCE-LEVEL AUTHORIZATION
================================

RBAC alone may be insufficient.

Authorization input may include:

```text
principal
organization
project
resource
action
repository trust
job risk
environment
```

Prepare a generic authorization decision boundary.

================================================================
22. POLICY-AS-CODE
==================

Introduce policy-as-code architecture.

Do NOT hard-depend on a policy engine simply for fashion.

Provide an interface such as:

```python
PolicyDecision:
    allow
    reason
    obligations
```

Potential backends:

```text
BuiltInPolicyEngine
ExternalOPA
```

if justified.

Verify current OPA documentation before implementing its integration.

================================================================
23. POLICY OBLIGATIONS
======================

Policy result should be capable of returning requirements such as:

```text
require_approval
require_security_review
max_budget
allowed_runtime
deny_secret_access
require_sandbox
```

Not just boolean ALLOW/DENY.

================================================================
24. POLICY VERSIONING
=====================

Store:

```text
policy version
policy hash
effective time
actor
```

Job/event audit should record which policy version produced a sensitive
decision.

================================================================
25. POLICY TESTING
==================

Provide:

```bash
zcoder policy test
```

or equivalent.

Policies must have unit tests.

A malformed security policy should fail closed.

================================================================
26. POLICY SIMULATION
=====================

Support non-mutating evaluation:

```bash
zcoder policy explain \
  --principal ... \
  --action job.approve \
  --resource ...
```

Show:

decision
matched rules
obligations

Do not expose secrets/internal tokens.

================================================================
27. ENTERPRISE SSO
==================

Existing OIDC should evolve into organization-aware SSO.

Support multiple OIDC provider configurations.

Each organization may configure an IdP.

Do not use one global issuer assumption.

================================================================
28. OIDC PROVIDER MODEL
=======================

Persist:

```text
issuer
client identifier
organization
claim mappings
domain restrictions
enabled status
```

Secrets must remain SecretRef values.

================================================================
29. OIDC DISCOVERY
==================

Where supported, use standards-compliant provider discovery.

Validate:

issuer
authorization endpoint
token endpoint
JWKS

Do not blindly trust discovery URLs supplied by unprivileged users.

Protect against SSRF.

================================================================
30. SSO DOMAIN CLAIMING
=======================

If organization domain verification is introduced:

do not trust an email domain merely because a user typed it.

Require verification workflow.

Do not automatically move existing users between organizations.

================================================================
31. ACCOUNT LINKING SECURITY
============================

Never link identities solely by email without an explicit secure policy.

Persist external identity:

```text
issuer
subject
```

as stable identity key.

================================================================
32. SCIM 2.0
============

Add enterprise provisioning boundary using current SCIM standards.

Implement only after re-reading current RFCs.

Initial resources:

```text
/Users
/Groups
```

Support appropriate:

GET
POST
PUT/PATCH
DELETE/deactivation
filtering
pagination

Do not invent vendor-specific SCIM semantics unless necessary.

================================================================
33. SCIM USER MAPPING
=====================

Map SCIM identity to ZCoder principals and memberships.

Do not make:

SCIM active=false

immediately destroy historical audit/job ownership.

Deactivate access while retaining referential history.

================================================================
34. SCIM GROUP MAPPING
======================

Allow SCIM groups to map to ZCoder organization/project roles according
to explicit configuration.

Do not let arbitrary external group names become admins automatically.

================================================================
35. SCIM AUTHENTICATION
=======================

SCIM endpoint authentication must use organization-scoped credential
or supported enterprise auth method.

Store only secure credential representation.

Never log bearer tokens.

================================================================
36. SCIM IDEMPOTENCY / VERSIONING
=================================

Handle retries safely.

Respect resource version semantics where implemented.

Do not duplicate users/groups when provisioning requests repeat.

================================================================
37. SCIM AUDIT
==============

Audit:

user provisioned
user deactivated
group changed
role mapping affected

Identify:

organization
SCIM client
resource
result

================================================================
38. SERVICE ACCOUNTS
====================

Introduce non-human principals.

Concept:

```python
ServiceAccount:
    id
    organization_id
    project_id optional
    name
    permissions/role
    status
```

Never model automated clients as fake human users.

================================================================
39. API KEYS
============

Support scoped API credentials.

API keys should have:

```text
id
prefix
secret
principal
scopes
created_at
expires_at
last_used_at
status
```

Persist only:

```text
secure hash
```

of secret material where architecture permits verification by hashing.

Display full key once.

================================================================
40. API KEY PREFIX
==================

Use non-secret identifiable prefix so operators can locate a key without
storing/displaying full value.

Logs may contain key ID/prefix, never secret.

================================================================
41. API KEY SCOPES
==================

Keys must not inherit unlimited organization permissions implicitly.

Support explicit scopes.

Example:

```text
job:read
job:write
repo:read
```

Authorization still obeys organization/project boundary.

================================================================
42. API KEY ROTATION
====================

Support:

create replacement
overlap period
revoke old

Do not require downtime.

================================================================
43. API KEY REVOCATION
======================

Revoked key must fail immediately or according to clearly documented
cache TTL.

Test cache invalidation.

================================================================
44. SERVICE-TO-SERVICE AUTH
===========================

Design machine identity independent from browser session auth.

Potentially support:

service-account API key
OAuth client flow where appropriate

Do not reuse browser cookies for worker/service authentication.

================================================================
45. WORKER IDENTITY
===================

Workers need authenticated machine identity.

A worker claiming jobs must prove identity and capabilities.

Do not trust self-reported:

worker_id
organization
capability

from unauthenticated processes.

================================================================
46. INTERNAL CONTROL-PLANE AUTH
===============================

Authenticate:

worker -> API
reconciler -> API
scheduler -> API

or use trusted direct DB architecture with explicit deployment boundary.

Document which services directly access PostgreSQL.

================================================================
47. USAGE METERING
==================

Create immutable usage events.

Concept:

```python
UsageEvent:
    id
    organization_id
    project_id
    job_id
    runtime
    provider
    model
    metric
    quantity
    unit
    occurred_at
    source
```

Do not derive financial usage only from current mutable job totals.

================================================================
48. USAGE SOURCES
=================

Differentiate:

```text
PROVIDER_REPORTED
RUNTIME_REPORTED
ZCODER_MEASURED
ESTIMATED
```

Never combine them silently.

================================================================
49. IDEMPOTENT METERING
=======================

Every billable usage event must have stable deduplication identity.

Reconnects/webhook retries must not double count usage.

================================================================
50. USAGE LEDGER
================

Provide append-oriented usage ledger.

Corrections should preferably use adjustment events rather than
rewriting historical usage without trace.

================================================================
51. USAGE AGGREGATION
=====================

Aggregate by:

```text
organization
project
repository
job
runtime
model
period
```

Keep raw ledger separate from materialized summaries.

================================================================
52. QUOTAS
==========

Implement scoped quotas.

Examples:

```text
monthly spend
daily spend
concurrent jobs
managed runtime hours
token usage
repository count
worker count
```

Do not make one universal quota model fit all units.

================================================================
53. QUOTA STATES
================

Support:

```text
OK
WARNING
SOFT_LIMIT
HARD_LIMIT
SUSPENDED
```

Separate:

notification threshold

from:

enforcement threshold.

================================================================
54. QUOTA ENFORCEMENT
=====================

Quota enforcement must occur before new expensive work begins.

Concurrent workers can race.

Use transactional reservation/allocation where necessary.

Do not rely only on:

current_usage < limit

without atomicity.

================================================================
55. USAGE RESERVATIONS
======================

For bounded operations, consider reservation:

```text
reserve budget
execute
settle actual
release unused
```

This can prevent concurrent overspend.

Do not pretend provider estimates are exact.

================================================================
56. PROVIDER HARD BUDGET VS ZCODER QUOTA
========================================

Keep distinct:

```text
provider hard budget
ZCoder organization quota
project quota
job budget
```

Document enforcement guarantees for each.

================================================================
57. BILLING BOUNDARY
====================

Create billing domain without forcing a payment vendor.

Concept:

```text
BillingAccount
Plan
Entitlement
UsageLedger
InvoicePeriod
```

Do not integrate Stripe/PayPal merely to claim billing support.

Payment processing can remain an adapter.

================================================================
58. ENTITLEMENTS
================

Plans should resolve to capabilities.

Examples:

```text
max_projects
max_repositories
managed_agents
advanced_policy
SCIM
audit_export
retention_days
```

Feature checks should use entitlement service, not scattered plan-name
comparisons.

Bad:

```python
if plan == "enterprise":
```

Preferred:

```python
entitlements.has("scim")
```

================================================================
59. PLAN VERSIONING
===================

Do not let editing a plan retroactively change historical billing
meaning.

Version entitlement/price definitions where needed.

================================================================
60. BILLING PERIOD
==================

Support deterministic time boundaries.

Be careful with:

timezone
month boundaries
late usage events

Store canonical timestamps in UTC.

================================================================
61. COST VS PRICE
=================

Distinguish:

```text
provider cost
platform internal cost
customer billable usage
customer price
```

They are not identical.

Do not expose internal cost assumptions as customer invoices accidentally.

================================================================
62. BILLING EXPORT
==================

Generate machine-readable usage/billing export.

Examples:

CSV
JSON

Do not require PDF invoices in this upgrade unless actual billing
requirements exist.

================================================================
63. SPEND ALERTS
================

Support thresholds:

50%
80%
100%

per organization/project.

Alert delivery should be idempotent per period/threshold.

================================================================
64. ORGANIZATION SUSPENSION
===========================

Suspension policy must define:

new jobs
running jobs
API access
read-only access
audit export
billing admin access

Do not lock an enterprise customer out of necessary audit/billing data
without policy.

================================================================
65. AUDIT EVENT MODEL
=====================

Upgrade audit trail into enterprise audit log.

Each record should include:

```text
event_id
organization_id
actor
actor_type
action
resource
result
timestamp
source_ip where appropriate
request_id
metadata
```

Avoid sensitive payloads.

================================================================
66. AUDIT IMMUTABILITY
======================

Normal application principals must not update/delete historical audit
records.

Use append-only application semantics.

Consider DB permissions/constraints.

Do not market this as cryptographic immutability unless implemented.

================================================================
67. AUDIT HASH CHAIN OPTIONAL
=============================

If tamper-evidence is desired, optionally implement chained hashes or
signed export manifests.

Do not claim tamper-proof.

Document threat model.

================================================================
68. AUDIT EXPORT
================

Support export by:

organization
time range
event type

Formats:

JSONL
CSV

Provide stable schema/version.

================================================================
69. SIEM EXPORT
===============

Create an adapter boundary for enterprise export.

Potential transports:

webhook
HTTP
object storage
syslog-like destination if justified

Do not hardcode Splunk/Datadog/etc as the domain model.

================================================================
70. AUDIT EXPORT RETRY
======================

Use durable outbox.

Failed export must not block primary control-plane transaction.

Provide retry/dead-letter visibility.

================================================================
71. DATA RETENTION
==================

Introduce configurable retention classes.

Examples:

```text
job events
agent logs
artifacts
audit events
usage ledger
SCIM history
```

Do not use one retention duration for all data.

================================================================
72. RETENTION LEGAL HOLD BOUNDARY
=================================

Design an extensible retention policy.

Do not claim legal-hold compliance unless actually implemented and
validated.

================================================================
73. ORGANIZATION DATA DELETION
==============================

Design safe asynchronous tenant deletion.

Pipeline:

request
approval if required
grace period
disable access
delete/cryptographically erase where possible
verify
audit completion

Do not delete shared provider resources belonging to another tenant.

================================================================
74. EXPORT BEFORE DELETE
========================

Optionally support customer data export before deletion.

Do not automatically expose secret/token material.

================================================================
75. OBJECT STORAGE TENANT BOUNDARY
==================================

If artifacts/backups/object storage are used, ensure tenant keys/path
layout cannot collide.

Never rely only on filename prefixes without authorization controls.

================================================================
76. ARTIFACT AUTHORIZATION
==========================

Downloading an artifact must re-check tenant/project permission.

Possession of an artifact ID must not grant access.

Use time-bounded signed URLs only where appropriate and scoped.

================================================================
77. CACHE TENANT ISOLATION
==========================

Audit caches.

Cache key must include tenant scope where data is tenant-specific.

Test:

A populates cache
B requests similar ID
B never receives A data

================================================================
78. OBSERVABILITY TENANT SAFETY
===============================

Logs/traces/metrics can leak tenant data.

Review:

repository names
task prompts
code paths
user emails
organization IDs

Metrics should use low-cardinality/internal dimensions.

Detailed tenant-level data belongs in authorized APIs/storage.

================================================================
79. ERROR MESSAGE ISOLATION
===========================

Cross-tenant lookup should avoid revealing whether inaccessible
resources exist.

Define consistent:

not found
forbidden

semantics according to threat model.

================================================================
80. RATE LIMITING
=================

Add quotas/rate limits at scopes:

principal
API key
organization
expensive endpoint

Do not allow one tenant to exhaust all API/control-plane capacity.

================================================================
81. FAIR SCHEDULING BETWEEN TENANTS
===================================

Extend scheduler fairness.

One organization should not monopolize workers.

Possible controls:

```text
org concurrency
project concurrency
weighted scheduling
```

Preserve priority policy.

================================================================
82. TENANT-AWARE WORKER ROUTING
===============================

High-security tenants may require:

dedicated workers
specific runtime
specific region
restricted network

Represent scheduling constraints.

Do not claim dedicated tenancy unless worker isolation is actually
enforced.

================================================================
83. DATA RESIDENCY POLICY BOUNDARY
==================================

Prepare organization/project policy for:

allowed execution regions
allowed provider regions
artifact residency

Do not claim regulatory residency based only on an LLM inference setting.

Infrastructure/database/object-storage locations also matter.

================================================================
84. REGION MODEL
================

Introduce region metadata without implementing multi-region yet.

Examples:

```text
control_plane_region
worker_region
provider_inference_region
data_region
```

Do not collapse them into one `region` value.

================================================================
85. MULTI-REGION ARCHITECTURE BOUNDARY
======================================

Design only.

Do not implement active-active multi-region unless explicitly proven.

Document:

what remains single-region
what state is globally authoritative
what must be replicated

================================================================
86. API VERSIONING
==================

Enterprise customers need API stability.

Define API versioning strategy.

Do not expose internal database models directly as public schemas.

================================================================
87. PAGINATION
==============

All enterprise list endpoints need bounded pagination.

Examples:

jobs
members
audit
usage
repositories

Avoid unbounded organization-wide queries.

================================================================
88. FILTERING / SORTING
=======================

Use allowlisted fields.

Never concatenate arbitrary sort/filter input into SQL.

================================================================
89. IDEMPOTENCY
===============

Mutation endpoints important to automation should support idempotency
where meaningful.

Examples:

job create
organization create
service-account create
billing export

================================================================
90. API ERROR CONTRACT
======================

Define stable machine-readable errors.

Concept:

```json
{
  "error": {
    "code": "...",
    "message": "...",
    "request_id": "..."
  }
}
```

Do not expose SQL/stack traces.

================================================================
91. ENTERPRISE API KEYS FOR AUTOMATION
======================================

Document:

creation
scope
rotation
expiration
revocation
audit

Never include full key in API list responses.

================================================================
92. SECURITY ADMIN EXPERIENCE
=============================

Provide commands/API for:

members
roles
service accounts
API keys
SSO
SCIM
policies
audit

Use shared services.

Do not implement business logic only in CLI.

================================================================
93. CLI TARGET
==============

Aim for coherent commands such as:

```bash
zcoder orgs list
zcoder orgs show ORG

zcoder projects list --org ORG

zcoder members list --org ORG

zcoder service-accounts list --org ORG
zcoder api-keys create ...

zcoder sso status --org ORG
zcoder scim status --org ORG

zcoder policy explain ...

zcoder usage show --org ORG
zcoder quotas show --org ORG

zcoder audit export ...
```

Follow existing CLI design if better.

================================================================
94. ADMIN WEB CONSOLE
=====================

Extend operations console.

Views:

```text
Organizations
Projects
Members
Groups
SSO
SCIM
Service Accounts
API Keys
Policies
Usage
Quotas
Audit
```

Sensitive operations require server-side authorization.

================================================================
95. ORGANIZATION SWITCHING
==========================

UI organization switching must update authenticated context safely.

Do not merely set tenant ID in browser local state and trust it.

================================================================
96. TENANT-SAFE WEBSOCKET/SSE
=============================

Realtime subscriptions must be authorized by tenant.

Test that reconnect/subscription token cannot access another tenant's
job stream.

================================================================
97. BACKGROUND JOB TENANT CONTEXT
=================================

Async worker jobs must carry signed/trusted internal tenant scope.

Do not accept tenant scope again from untrusted task payload later in
execution.

================================================================
98. OUTBOX TENANT SCOPE
=======================

Outbox messages must contain validated tenant/resource correlation.

Outbox worker must re-check appropriate policy for delayed dangerous
actions when required.

================================================================
99. WEBHOOK TENANT ROUTING
==========================

GitHub webhook:

installation
→ repository
→ ZCoder organization/project

Mapping must be authoritative.

Do not route by repository name alone.

================================================================
100. GITHUB INSTALLATION REASSIGNMENT
=====================================

If administrators move/reassociate installation mappings, ensure existing
jobs/audit retain historical tenant identity.

Do not silently transfer historical data.

================================================================
101. GITHUB APP CROSS-TENANT TESTS
==================================

Installation A must never mutate repositories assigned to tenant B.

Test intentionally malicious identifiers.

================================================================
102. SCIM CROSS-TENANT TESTS
============================

SCIM credential for Organization A must never provision/deactivate users
in Organization B.

P0 test.

================================================================
103. API KEY CROSS-TENANT TESTS
===============================

Project-scoped key:

must not access another project.

Organization-scoped key:

must not access another organization.

P0.

================================================================
104. AUDIT CROSS-TENANT TESTS
=============================

Security Auditor A must not query audit records B.

Even if numeric/event IDs are guessed.

================================================================
105. USAGE CROSS-TENANT TESTS
=============================

Billing admin A must not view B's cost/usage.

Test aggregate endpoints, not just individual rows.

================================================================
106. POLICY CROSS-TENANT TESTS
==============================

A tenant admin cannot install or alter global policy.

A project admin cannot weaken organization immutable policy.

================================================================
107. RLS BYPASS TEST
====================

Create a test specifically demonstrating that the normal application
role cannot bypass RLS through ownership or BYPASSRLS privilege.

This must run against real PostgreSQL.

================================================================
108. MIGRATION + RLS TEST
=========================

Run migrations as migration role.

Then switch to application role.

Verify RLS remains active.

Do not accidentally leave migrated tables owned by an application role
that bypasses intended isolation.

================================================================
109. BACKUP RESTORE TENANCY TEST
================================

Backup enterprise dataset containing at least two organizations.

Restore.

Verify:

organization IDs
RLS
memberships
usage
audit
policies

remain isolated.

================================================================
110. LOAD TEST WITH MULTIPLE TENANTS
====================================

Use FakeRuntime.

Simulate multiple organizations concurrently.

Measure:

queue fairness
DB contention
API latency
usage aggregation
rate limiting

Do not use live Anthropic calls for load testing.

================================================================
111. NOISY NEIGHBOR TEST
========================

Organization A floods job queue.

Organization B submits normal work.

Verify configured fairness/limits prevent total starvation.

================================================================
112. QUOTA RACE TEST
====================

Multiple workers simultaneously start operations near a tenant hard
limit.

Prove aggregate quota cannot be exceeded because of a race beyond the
explicitly documented tolerance.

Use real PostgreSQL.

================================================================
113. USAGE DUPLICATION TEST
===========================

Replay:

runtime event
webhook
reconnect

Prove billable usage is not counted twice.

================================================================
114. BILLING ADJUSTMENT TEST
============================

Create initial usage then an adjustment.

Historical ledger remains traceable.

Aggregate reflects correction.

================================================================
115. IDENTITY TEST MATRIX
=========================

Test:

valid OIDC
invalid issuer
invalid audience
expired token
unknown subject
removed membership
suspended organization
multiple organization membership
SCIM deactivated principal
revoked API key
expired API key

================================================================
116. OAUTH/OIDC SECURITY REVIEW
===============================

Re-check current OAuth/OIDC security best practices from standards
sources before finalizing implementation.

Do not preserve legacy insecure flows merely because they are easy.

================================================================
117. SSRF REVIEW
================

Enterprise-configurable:

OIDC issuer
webhook destinations
policy endpoints
SIEM export

may create SSRF surfaces.

Validate destination schemes/addresses according to security policy.

Protect metadata/private network endpoints.

================================================================
118. SSO CONFIG APPROVAL
========================

Changing enterprise identity configuration can lock out an organization.

Require elevated role.

Audit old/new configuration metadata safely.

Consider break-glass behavior.

================================================================
119. SCIM TOKEN ROTATION
========================

Allow SCIM credential rotation without losing provisioning continuity.

Audit credential lifecycle.

================================================================
120. TENANT CREATION TRANSACTION
================================

Creating organization should atomically create required:

organization
default project if desired
owner membership
policy baseline
billing account/usage scope

Do not leave partially initialized organizations.

================================================================
121. DEFAULT-DENY TENANT POLICY
===============================

If tenant scope is missing unexpectedly:

fail closed.

Never interpret missing tenant as:

all tenants.

================================================================
122. GLOBAL ADMIN
=================

If platform-level administration exists:

make it a distinct privileged principal type.

Do not give ordinary OrganizationOwner global access.

All global-admin accesses to tenant data should be heavily audited.

================================================================
123. IMPERSONATION
==================

Do not add tenant impersonation casually.

If implemented for support:

explicit reason
short duration
visible audit
strong role requirement

Never silently impersonate.

================================================================
124. SUPPORT ACCESS
===================

Design support access as controlled workflow.

Default:

no tenant data access.

Do not assume SaaS operator automatically reads customer source code.

================================================================
125. DATA MINIMIZATION
======================

Review whether ZCoder actually needs to persist:

full prompts
source snippets
tool output
GitHub logs

Allow retention/minimization controls.

================================================================
126. ENCRYPTION BOUNDARY
========================

Document encryption assumptions:

in transit
database/storage
backup
object storage

Do not claim field-level encryption unless implemented.

================================================================
127. CUSTOMER-MANAGED KEYS
==========================

Architecture boundary only unless actually required.

Do not claim BYOK/CMK support because cloud storage is encrypted.

================================================================
128. AUDIT SCHEMA VERSIONING
============================

Audit exports need stable schema version.

Consumers must be able to evolve safely.

================================================================
129. SCIM CONFORMANCE DOCUMENT
==============================

Document supported:

resources
attributes
filters
patch operations
pagination
limitations

Do not claim full SCIM compliance without conformance evidence.

================================================================
130. ENTERPRISE FEATURE MATRIX
==============================

Create:

```text
docs/ENTERPRISE-FEATURE-MATRIX.md
```

Columns:

```text
Feature
Implemented
Tested
Live-tested
Standard
Limitations
Entitlement
```

No marketing-only checkmarks.

================================================================
131. THREAT MODEL
=================

Expand threat model with:

cross-tenant read
cross-tenant mutation
tenant-ID spoofing
RLS bypass
pooled connection contamination
malicious SCIM client
stolen API key
malicious org admin
billing manipulation
usage duplication
audit tampering
SSRF via enterprise configuration
noisy neighbor
support/admin abuse

================================================================
